<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	App Sandbox is on. The WoW installation (under /Applications) and the
	sync folder (normally inside iCloud Drive) are both outside the
	sandbox container, so the app asks the user to pick each one once via
	NSOpenPanel and keeps a security-scoped bookmark to it afterward — see
	FolderAccess.swift / CloudFolderAccess. These two entitlements are what
	let those grants keep working across launches.

	Deliberately NOT using the iCloud capability
	(com.apple.developer.icloud-container-identifiers /
	com.apple.developer.icloud-services): that requires a paid Apple
	Developer Program membership, and a free "Personal Team" can build an
	app that claims it but the OS refuses to launch the result (a
	RunningBoard/code-signing failure at launch, not a build error). The
	sync folder the user picks can simply live inside their regular iCloud
	Drive instead — no special entitlement needed for that, it works the
	same as dragging any other file into iCloud Drive in Finder.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.files.bookmarks.app-scope</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Outbound network access for the WowInterface API. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
